// Solution — Module 06, Exercise 03: The + 1
//
// Task 1: the two dumps are
//
//   0008  48 45 4c 50  00      |HELP.|     <- src, 5 bytes, correct
//   0010  48 45 4c 50          |HELP|      <- wrong, 4 bytes, not a string
//
// Every letter in `wrong` is correct. The copy loop did exactly what it was
// asked. There is no corruption, no garbage, nothing to see in a debugger —
// the block simply stops one byte before the thing that says it stopped.
//
// Task 2: uncommenting `wrong[need] = 0` gives
//
//   area does not exist at line 57, column 1
//   byte 4..4 is outside this pine's block, which covers 0..3
//
// need is 4, the block covers 0..3, so index 4 is the first byte past the
// end. The terminator has nowhere to go. That is what `malloc(strlen(s))`
// buys you: a buffer that fits the text and cannot hold the string.
//
// Task 3: strlen(wrong) walks off the end and faults, exactly as in
// Exercise 02 — it reads 0..3, finds no zero, and asks for byte 4. In C it
// would keep going into the next allocation and return whatever it found.
//
// Task 4: this one has a trap in it.
//
// crunch-c zeroes every block it hands out, so a fresh `right` dumps as
// 00 00 00 00 00 and strlen(right) is 0 — a valid empty string, by accident.
// C's malloc guarantees nothing of the kind. It hands you whatever those
// bytes last held, so the same code in C reads uninitialised memory and
// returns a length that depends on the heap's history. (calloc zeroes; malloc
// does not, and the difference is exactly this bug.)
//
// So: being correctly sized and being initialised are two separate problems.
// The + 1 solves the first. Only writing a terminator solves the second, and
// crunch-c's zeroing is a courtesy you must not learn to depend on.

fn strlen(p) {
    n = 0
    while p[n] != 0 {
        n = n + 1
    }
    return n
}

src = garrison(5)
src[0] = 72   // H
src[1] = 69   // E
src[2] = 76   // L
src[3] = 80   // P
src[4] = 0

need = strlen(src)
print(f"strlen(src) = {need}, so the buffer needs {need} + 1 = {need + 1} bytes")
bathysphere(src)

// Task 1: the wrong allocation, filled correctly.
wrong = garrison(need)
i = 0
while i < need {
    wrong[i] = src[i]
    i = i + 1
}
print("")
bathysphere(wrong)
print("all four letters present, and it is still not a string")

// Task 4: the right size, never written to.
print("")
right = garrison(need + 1)
bathysphere(right)
print(f"fresh block, zeroed by crunch-c - strlen says {strlen(right)}")
print("in C that block would hold whatever was there before, and strlen")
print("would return a number that depends on the heap's history.")

scorch(src)
scorch(right)

// Task 2: the terminator that does not fit. Ends the program, so it goes
// last.
print("")
print(f"now writing a terminator at index {need} of a {need}-byte block...")
wrong[need] = 0
print("unreachable")
